Z||000000||Polarbear||07-27-2001||01:21 PM||||I need some HELP!!!<P>I saw the perfect Fiero almost 4 ½ month ago. It just needed to be fixed here and there said the car-dealer, but after two months he still hadn’t fixed it. I decided to by it anyway (at a discount), and sent it to a repair shop that the car-dealer recommended. But it was exactly the same with the repair shop. The owner kept promising to look at it, and said he had repaired stuff. But when i came to check on it the repairs hadn’t been done, and he said that he was just about ready to start on it. <P>Then the original car-dealer suggested that I got it repaired in Germany at a guy called Charles Shepard, and he could help me with the transport. Now guess what... I moved the car to his place, and now he hasn’t time to help me, and said I have to arrange everything myself.<P>I’m so frustrated! I’m going MAD!!!<P>Please, is there somebody who knows of a good autorepair in Denmark, Southern Sweden or Northern Germany who knows Fieros.<P>There isn’t much wrong with the Fiero: The Engine service comes on after 15 minutes and it doesn’t take Gas propably. Some other minor things has to be fixed. I’m not a mechanic so its not something I can handle myself<P>Help!<P>Polarbear||||reg||8
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||07-27-2001||01:46 PM||||<BR>Polarbear, I am in the states and don't have a clue about Euproean auto shops.<P>But, if you can follow instructions, are willing to buy some tools, and get your hands dirty, I'm willing to take the stance that you can work on the Fiero.<P>First what you need to do is determine what codes the computer is giving you.  This will help you determine why the fiero is running poorly.<P>To access the diagnosis terminal and check the codes, you need to remove the lighter cover plate on the console.  You need a Torx 15 driver to remove the screws, but you may be able to do it with something else.<P>Once you have the plate removed the diagnosis terminal is on the right side of the console.  At this point, you need a metal paper clip, and what you are going to do is connect the bottom right two connectors.<P>The fiero should be turned off while you are doing this.  Once you have the connectors jumped together, turn on the iginition. Do not start the car.  Your Service Engine Soon (SES) light will flash at you.<P>It will flash once, then twice. So it goes like this: flash... flash flash... this sequence will repeat two more times.  This is the eqivalent to a code 12, which means the system is normal.  Now, since your computer is illuminating the SES light during actual run time of the fiero, there are some stored codes.<P>After the flash of 12, you will receive the stored computer codes.  For instance, you may receive a flash flash flash... flash flash... this is the eqivalent of 32.  Keep in mind that all codes will be display in sets of 3.<P>So, when checking the codes, the computer starts with 12, returns any and all stored codes, then ends with 12 to indicate the end of the diagnosis.<P>Determine your codes, then post them on the board to get further help.<P>BTW, welcome to the forum.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Not So Fair Weather Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A><P>Edited for sentence structure and some content.<p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 07-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Polarbear||07-27-2001||04:15 PM||||Thanks for the input,<P>There are several reasons why I don’t start working on it myself. Primarely I don’t have the time, don’t have anywhere to do it, and would like to drive it this year  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. <P>Also the first car-dealer spent 2 years looking at the problem with the engine, and the first repair-shop had the professional equipment to read out all the codes from the engine. They replaced some parts, but it didn’t help. What are the chances that I’m going to get it running…<P>I *think* it was an “EGR” problem, because I just received the bill from him for the spare parts he used. He told me I didn´t have to pay for his worktime (Doh! Like I was going to pay him for doing nothing for 1 ½ month…).  It is a strange thing. All the people I have delt with has been really nice, but they just don’t get the job done... <P>Thanks for the info on the codes, though I had already figured that one out. I have never tested it though, cause I never had the Fiero home yet…<P>I think the best solution is to let a professional take a look at it.<P>Nice home page, btw!||||reg||7
Z||000003||87GTZ34||07-27-2001||04:41 PM||||Polarbear, I know its probably not feasible but...  ship it to me & I will make all the necessary repairs to make it a reliable daily driver.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gold-86SE||07-27-2001||05:29 PM||||<BR>Polarbear, thanks for the complement.<P>I was thinking that if the EGR is the only code that is being given, leave it be for now, unless you guys have emissions testing.<P>As for your fuel, it may be a fouled fuel filter. The fuel filter is located on the passenger side of the fiero, near the rear wheel, strapped to underside of the frame.<P>It is also possible that the fuel pressure may not be high enough for the engine to run properly.  The fuel pressure should be about 40, if it is several marks under that, like in the high 20s, then that most likely is a portion of the problem.<P>One thing you might tell these guys working on the fiero is that if they are family with any GM product they should be able to work with the fiero.<P>Good luck.<BR>||||reg||1
